cartridge "FLAG HUNT/JOHN FEDOR/1989"

' FLAG HUNT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Flag Hunt" by John Fedor, Ahoy! January
' 1989 (Commodore 64, machine code; disk files FLAG HUNT and FLAG
' HUNT.ML). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026,
' in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/flaghunt-ml.dis), the game is rebuilt for the
' ColecoVision with tile graphics, smooth sprites and sound.
'
' The game: two players on five floors joined by ladders. Green's flag
' stands on the top floor, Red's on the bottom one. Run to the other
' side's flag, take it (walk onto it) and bring it back to your own flag
' to capture it. A shot (or touching the other player) sends a player
' back home, and a carried flag goes back to its place.
'
' From the original (orig/): five floors, seven ladders (two, one, two,
' two between the floors, at the C64's places scaled to 28 columns);
' players climb only on a ladder and walk only on a floor; green starts
' on the top floor beside its flag, red on the bottom floor beside its
' flag; a flag is taken by standing on its spot and captured by standing
' on your own flag's spot with it (your own flag need not be home, as on
' the C64); shots fly in eight directions through the floors, one shot
' per player at a time, until they leave the screen; a shot or the two
' players touching sends the player(s) home and drops the carried flag
' back on its spot; the C64's flag blinked on its spot and vanished when
' taken.
'
' Liberties: a computer can play red (ONE VS COMPUTER); a game is won by
' the first to three captures (the C64 game ended at the first capture);
' a player's own shot never hits him (on the C64 a shot starting inside
' his sprite could); a shot that hits stops; a button fires in the stick's
' direction, or the way the player faces when the stick is centred (the
' C64 fired only with a direction, holding the button); the carried flag
' is shown over the carrier's head; speeds are in sixtieths of a second (a
' pixel a frame walking and climbing, three for a shot: the C64's loop had
' no frame sync); taking a flag or a ladder allows a few pixels of slack
' (the C64 needed the exact pixel); the flags no longer blink (it was
' faster than 3 times a second); sounds and music are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Stick: walk on a floor, climb on a ladder.
' Either side button: shoot. Player 1 (green) uses controller 1, player 2
' (red) controller 2; against the computer, either controller. On the
' title, up and down choose, left, right or a button change the players,
' a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`.

